{
	title: 'Lazy history: the tail first, the rest on demand',
	spec: `Opening a session costs what is shown, not the size of its history. A snapshot carries the transcript of the history's tail only (the last records up to a byte budget, about 256 KB, never cutting a turn in two) plus everything the state needs from earlier (an open question, the last prompt for editing, inbox, draft, usage), and says whether earlier records exist. A client gets earlier records with a \`history\` command { sessionId, before } answered in pages. The terminal paints the tail at once and then fetches the rest in the background, repainting once when it has all of it (full-mode rules of tasks/cc/terminal.md hold: the frame still ends up with every history line). The web prepends earlier pages when the reader scrolls near the top, keeping what they were reading in place. After first paint, both clients load the other open tabs' tails in background, nearest focused tab first, one snapshot per event-loop slice; the tab bar entries still come from the tabs event. A switch to a loaded tab uses its cached transcript immediately. Background loading is not intentionally throttled after startup.
On the host nothing reads every session or every history eagerly: listing tabs reads only tabs.ason and the open sessions' metadata; the newest session is found from directory names; recovering unfinished turns reads tails (history.tail already does); provider input is rebuilt from the full history only when a turn runs, then kept in memory while the session is open.
The host never answers a big request in one synchronous go (task 7j): pages.ts reads history as steps (generators that pause before decoding each record), run at once for small reads (up to about 64 KB) and otherwise in slices of about 5 ms, shared by all sliced work in one event-loop turn and yielding (setImmediate) between them; a record too big for what is left of a slice starts the next. Snapshots, \`history\` pages, catching up marks and the open tabs' states at connect (their marks and essential records, before the tabs event) all go through it. While a client's open is being read, its later commands for that session wait and then run in the order sent; a tail read in slices is caught up with records appended meanwhile (pages.since) in the same step that sends the snapshot, so no live event is missed or doubled. The in-memory connection does not copy the history records of a snapshot or page through ASON (host/wire.ts; they are freshly read and shared with nobody). The tab bar reuses each tab's state derived from history until its marks grow.
Tests: a snapshot read in slices holds a record appended meanwhile, commands sent after the open are answered after its snapshot and in order, and sliced pages yield every record once. A snapshot of a 20 MB history reads at most the budget from disk (count bytes read), holds the tail items and an open question from far back; paging back yields every record exactly once and in order; the terminal's frame after the background load equals the frame from a full load. Applying fetched pages compares them with the snapshot's stand-ins only while earlier pages remain (the web's per-page apply); the terminal's one apply of all pages never stringifies them (that cost 100+ ms on a 50 MB history, task 7j). Startup recovery also catches up each open or busy session's marks through pages.slices before deciding whether it has unfinished work: the first-frame fallback may start recovery while tab indexing is still in progress, and recovery must never finish that indexing synchronously through pages.marks.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'history.tail() reads the last 64 KiB and falls back to a full readSync when the last line is longer; with images in history (task 2a stores blob refs, not bytes) lines stay small, but a single huge tool result can still trigger the fallback. Page by byte offsets from the end, not by record counts from the start.',
		'replay.current(records) applies prompt replacement (task wb): an edited prompt supersedes records before the tail boundary too. The snapshot must fold through current() over enough history to be right, or send the replacement as part of the tail.',
		'old-session-loader.ts and old-background-loader.ts show the old approach (entryLimit for the focused tab, background loading of the others). The old Hal loaded all open tabs in the background after the first paint; Hal loads a tab when it is shown.',
		'Built: src/host/pages.ts (tail, pages by byte offset, marks), src/common/backfill.ts (client paging), transcript.standIns/prepend. An open question can sit behind megabytes of command output, so reading back from the end cannot stay within the budget: sessions/<id>/marks.ason keeps the offsets of the last question, answer or turn end, turn record, prompt and undelivered inbox messages. It is a cache: its `size` says how much history it covers, the rest is read and folded in, and a missing or too-long one is rebuilt from the whole file.',
		'Pages (and the snapshot tail) start at a non-replacing prompt when one is in reach. That makes chunks fold independently (an answer always comes before the next prompt; an edit sits with the prompt it replaces), so transcript.prepend can put page items in front of a live transcript. Stand-ins (the open question, the prompt of a turn the tail cuts) stay on top until a page holds them.',
		"history.open no longer reads the whole file: it repairs only the end, so a corrupt record further back is reported when read (readSync, a snapshot), not at open. readSync keeps each open session's records in memory and reads only what was appended since; tests that simulate a host restart must call history.stop(false) as the real exit handler does, or the in-memory turn never reaches disk.",
		"Web: transcript.prepend puts page items in front, so every item's index shifts. The web transcript keyed rows by position (task 4s), which is only safe while items append: after a page lands each card is rebound to another item and open cards move with their slot. Rows need a key that survives prepending, and the 4s render test loads a page and asserts the cards already shown stay connected with their open state.",
		'Task a7 supersedes the old note saying background tabs wait until shown: both clients now prefetch all open tabs tails after focused paint, keep live subscriptions, and switch to cached transcripts. Full earlier-page backfill still starts only when a terminal tab is focused or the web requests it.'
	],
	needs: ['7w', '4s']
}
